---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import market from '../data/market.json';
import { ETFS } from '../data/etfs.mjs';
import { longDate } from '../lib/format';

const generated = market.generatedAt.slice(0, 10);
---

<Base title="Methodology" description="Where the data comes from, how the pipeline works and the exact formulas behind every indicator on the site.">
  <section class="intro">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow">Methodology</div>
      <h1>No black box: <span class="gradient-text">every number is explained.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Everything on this site is computed by code written for it: no charting library, no indicator package. This
        page documents the data, the pipeline and each formula, so any figure can be checked by hand.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>1. The universe</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        Six products, all issued by Amundi, listed on Euronext Paris and quoted in euros: what a French retail investor
        can actually buy. All are accumulating (dividends are reinvested), so price performance is close to total return.
      </p>
      <div class="panel table-scroll">
        <table class="data">
          <thead><tr><th>Product</th><th>Ticker</th><th>ISIN</th><th>Index</th><th>Replication</th><th>Sleeve</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              ETFS.map((e) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{e.name}</td>
                  <td class="num">{e.ticker}</td>
                  <td class="num">{e.isin}</td>
                  <td>{e.index}</td>
                  <td>{e.replication}</td>
                  <td>{e.sleeve}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="faint small">
        Product data (ISIN, index, listing) should always be checked against the issuer's official documents (KID,
        factsheet) on amundietf.fr before any decision.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>2. The data pipeline</h2>
      <div class="pipeline">
        <div class="step panel"><span class="n">1</span><h3>Schedule</h3><p>A GitHub Actions job runs every weekday evening, after the Euronext close.</p></div>
        <div class="step panel"><span class="n">2</span><h3>Fetch</h3><p>Two years of daily open, high, low, close and volume per product, from Euronext's official historical prices (Yahoo Finance as fallback).</p></div>
        <div class="step panel"><span class="n">3</span><h3>Validate</h3><p>Currency must be EUR, missing days dropped, duplicates removed, any daily move above 25% rejected as bad data.</p></div>
        <div class="step panel"><span class="n">4</span><h3>Compute</h3><p>Indicators, performance, risk, correlations and the text summaries, written to one JSON file.</p></div>
        <div class="step panel"><span class="n">5</span><h3>Build &amp; deploy</h3><p>Astro renders static pages and charts; GitHub Pages serves them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">
        <strong>Fail-safe by design.</strong> If a series cannot be fetched from either source or fails validation, the
        job stops and the previous version of the site stays online: stale data is shown with its date, wrong data is
        never published. Last data file generated on {longDate(generated)}.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap formulas">
      <h2>3. Indicators and formulas</h2>
      <p class="muted">Notation: <code>C<sub>t</sub></code> is the close of session <code>t</code>, <code>n</code> the period.</p>

      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3>Simple moving average (SMA 20, 50, 200)</h3>
          <div class="formula">SMA<sub>n</sub>(t) = (C<sub>t</sub> + C<sub>t-1</sub> + … + C<sub>t-n+1</sub>) / n</div>
          <p class="muted">The average close of the last n sessions. It smooths out daily noise; the 200-session average is the classic long-term trend line.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3>Exponential moving average (EMA)</h3>
          <div class="formula">EMA(t) = α·C<sub>t</sub> + (1 − α)·EMA(t−1), &nbsp;α = 2 / (n + 1)</div>
          <p class="muted">Weights recent prices more. Seeded with the SMA of the first n values. Used inside the MACD.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3>RSI (14), Wilder 1978</h3>
          <div class="formula">
            G = avg gain, L = avg loss over 14 sessions<br />
            G(t) = (13·G(t−1) + gain<sub>t</sub>) / 14 &nbsp;(same for L)<br />
            RSI = 100 − 100 / (1 + G / L)
          </div>
          <p class="muted">Between 0 and 100. Above 70: overbought (a fast rise). Below 30: oversold (a fast fall). Tested against Wilder's published worked example.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3>MACD (12, 26, 9), Appel</h3>
          <div class="formula">
            MACD = EMA<sub>12</sub>(C) − EMA<sub>26</sub>(C)<br />
            Signal = EMA<sub>9</sub>(MACD)<br />
            Histogram = MACD − Signal
          </div>
          <p class="muted">Measures momentum. A positive histogram means short-term momentum is improving relative to its recent average.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3>Volatility (annualised, 3 months)</h3>
          <div class="formula">r<sub>t</sub> = ln(C<sub>t</sub> / C<sub>t−1</sub>), &nbsp;σ<sub>ann</sub> = stdev(r over 63 sessions) × √252</div>
          <p class="muted">How much the price typically moves. 15% means a one-standard-deviation year is roughly ±15%.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3>Max drawdown and correlation</h3>
          <div class="formula">
            DD(t) = C<sub>t</sub> / max(C<sub>0..t</sub>) − 1, &nbsp;MDD = min DD<br />
            ρ(A,B) = cov(r<sub>A</sub>, r<sub>B</sub>) / (σ<sub>A</sub>·σ<sub>B</sub>)
          </div>
          <p class="muted">Drawdown: the worst peak-to-trough loss over 12 months. Correlation: Pearson, on daily log returns aligned by date over 252 sessions.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub">Performance windows</h3>
      <p class="muted">
        1M, 3M and 1Y compare the last close with the close 21, 63 and 252 sessions earlier. YTD compares it with the last
        close of the previous calendar year. These are price returns of accumulating funds, before any brokerage fees
        or taxes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>4. How the automatic summaries are written</h2>
      <p class="muted">Each ETF's "What the indicators say" box is generated by fixed rules, not by a human and not by AI:</p>
      <div class="panel table-scroll">
        <table class="data rules">
          <thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Uptrend</td><td>Close &gt; SMA 50 &gt; SMA 200</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Downtrend</td><td>Close &lt; SMA 50 &lt; SMA 200</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mixed</td><td>Any other ordering</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Golden / death cross</td><td>SMA 50 crossed above / below SMA 200 within the last 20 sessions</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Overbought / oversold</td><td>RSI ≥ 70 / RSI ≤ 30</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Momentum improving / fading</td><td>MACD histogram &gt; 0 / &lt; 0</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Candle pattern</td><td>Engulfing, doji (body &lt; 10% of range), hammer / shooting star (wick ≥ 2× body), strong candle (body &gt; 80% of range), checked on the last session</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>5. Limits</h2>
      <ul class="limits muted">
        <li>Technical indicators describe the past. They have no proven ability, on their own, to predict future prices.</li>
        <li>Market prices of an ETF can differ slightly from its net asset value (premium or discount), especially at the open.</li>
        <li>Daily data from a free source can contain errors or gaps; the validation step reduces but does not eliminate this risk.</li>
        <li>Nothing here takes into account fees, taxes, or anyone's personal situation.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .intro { padding: 64px 0 40px; }
  .intro h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.4vw, 3rem); max-width: 22ch; }
  .small { font-size: 0.82rem; margin-top: 10px; }
  .pipeline { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(190px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0; counter-reset: s; }
  .step { position: relative; padding-top: 18px; }
  .step .n {
    display: inline-grid; place-items: center; width: 28px; height: 28px; border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--grad); color: #140b1f; font: 700 0.85rem var(--mono); margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .step p { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0; }
  .formulas .panel p { font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 0; }
  .sub { margin-top: 32px; }
  .rules td { text-align: left !important; white-space: normal; }
  .limits li { margin-bottom: 8px; }
</style>
